    "lawCode": "Amd §131-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6443\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto exempting income earned by persons under the age of twenty-four from\ncertain workforce development programs from the determination of need\nfor public assistance programs\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would amend the social services law to exclude income of an\nindividual up to the age of twenty-four earned from their participation\nin the summer youth employment program from the calculation of family\nincome for the purposes of public assistance.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2018, about 75,000 young people gained valuable work experience\nthrough the Summer Youth Employment Program (SYEP). However, for certain\nyoung people whose families rely on public benefits such as Temporary\nAssistance for Needy Families (TANF), their family's benefit could be\nreduced as a result of the additional income from SYEP. This reduction\ncould have a serious impact on some families, and make them reluctant to\nallow their children to participate in career-enhancing programs. The\nCity therefore proposes that income from SYEP and certain other subsi-\ndized youth workforce development programs, such as the federally-funded\nIn-School and Out-of-School Youth program, be exempt from the family's\nincome for the purpose of calculating public benefits.\n\nUnder current law, a young person's earnings from the Summer Yotith\nEmployment Program are exempt only if the youth resides in a family with\ndependent children and is in school at least part-time.  Workforce\ndevelopment programs may serve youth up to age 24. The American Recovery\nand Reinvestment Act (ARRA) of 2009, which stipulated that youth up to\nage 24 were eligible to receive ARRA-subsidized wages through summer\nyouth employment programs, provided a. basis for offering such programs\nto this age group, and New York City has since served youth through age\n24 though SYEP. In addition, the Workforce Innovation and Opportunity\nAct (WIOA) of 2014 raised the age of eligibility for Out-of-School youth\nprograms to the age of 24. The City proposes to amend SSL 131-a to\nprovide an exemption for all the earnings from the Summer Youth Employ-\nment Program and other subsidized youth workforce development programs\nrun by two of the City's social service agencies, regardless of the\npresence of dependent children in the household or whether the youth is\nin school. This would provide a stronger incentive for a young person to\nenroll in a youth workforce development program, because her earnings\nwould have no negative impact on her family's public assistance. Addi-\ntionally, if a young person is living on her own, potentially with her\nown children to support, this exemption would also make sure her own\nbenefits are not hurt by her choice to take advantage of a youth-orient-\ned workforce development program.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
